Fork CLUSTER_WORKERS_COUNT workers instead of one per host CPU

cluster.js passes no `workersCount` to graceful-cluster, which then forks
`os.cpus().length` workers: the CPU count of the host, not the container's CPU
limit. On a large node one container starts dozens of Node processes, each
holding its own plugin set and cache.

`CLUSTER_WORKERS_COUNT` sets the count; config.local.js reads it from
`IFRAMELY_WORKERS_COUNT` and defaults to 2. Unset, graceful-cluster keeps its
own behaviour.

Covered by `test/cluster-workers.test.js`, which boots cluster.js and counts
the workers it forks.
Index: b/lib/iframely/cluster.js
===================================================================
--- a/lib/iframely/cluster.js
+++ b/lib/iframely/cluster.js
@@ -12,6 +12,7 @@ GracefulCluster.start({
     disableGraceful: CONFIG.DEBUG,
     restartOnTimeout: CONFIG.CLUSTER_WORKER_RESTART_ON_PERIOD,
     restartOnMemory: CONFIG.CLUSTER_WORKER_RESTART_ON_MEMORY_USED,
+    workersCount: CONFIG.CLUSTER_WORKERS_COUNT,
     serverFunction: function() {
         import('./server.js');
     }
